\section{Let Firing Rules Depend on Data}

Dynamic dataflow permits token rates or enabled actions to depend on runtime values or actor state. The compiler can know the set of ports and actions without knowing a complete periodic schedule. A guard is part of the firing rule: inspecting an input to test a guard must not accidentally consume it if another action is selected. If several actions are enabled, priority declarations or a specified choice semantics determine whether the actor is deterministic.

Tags solve a different problem. In a reentrant or iterative graph, a token can carry a frame, iteration, or invocation identity so that a matching node combines operands from the same dynamic context. A tag does not choose a guarded action, and a guard does not identify which invocation produced a token. Conflating them creates cross-iteration matches.

\section{Route Values With CAL Guards}

This CAL actor has two actions with complementary guards:

\begin{verbatim}
actor Split() int I ==> int O1, int O2 :
  A: action I:[val] ==> O1:[val]
     guard val >= 0 end
  B: action I:[val] ==> O2:[val]
     guard val < 0 end
end
\end{verbatim}

Each firing consumes one input token, but the selected output port depends on its value. Thus no fixed nonzero production rate exists for either output individually. If dynamic instances are tagged, input \(({-3},k)\) must produce \(({-3},k)\) on \texttt{O2}; the guard changes the route, not the context tag. \Cref{fig:visual-357} displays both decisions.

