% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Choose an Action Atomically}

\begin{verbatim}
snapshot := peek_required_input_prefixes(actor)
candidates := actions_whose_patterns_match(snapshot)
enabled := [a for a in candidates if evaluate(a.guard, snapshot, state)]
a := select_by_declared_priority_or_choice_semantics(enabled)
atomically:
    consume(a.input_pattern)
    update(a.explicit_state)
    emit(a.output_pattern, preserving_context_tags)
\end{verbatim}

The atomic region is a semantic event even if a lock-free implementation realizes it differently. Evaluating every action body speculatively would be wrong when bodies update actor state or perform effects.

\section{Retain Dynamic Evidence in the IR}

The IR should contain actor and action nodes, typed FIFO edges, input/output patterns, guard and priority edges, tag schemas, actor state, variable rates, effects, and source provenance. Clocks or rate intervals may be recorded, but unknown dynamic rates must not be replaced by convenient constants. Compilation may produce a guarded actor runtime, state-machine code, hardware arbitration, or profiling instrumentation and execution-trace graphs.

Legal optimizations include folding a proven guard, deleting an unreachable action, or fusing actors when the composed action rules preserve all token sequences. Semantic-preservation risks include consuming a token while probing a losing guard, dropping tags, reordering state updates, and treating a profiled action frequency as a semantic rate. Dynamic traces can guide optimization, but one trace does not prove that an unobserved guard is impossible.

\section{Match Dynamic Instances Before Firing a Join}

Downstream from a router, suppose two channels each carry responses for several concurrently active requests. A join must pair responses for the same request, not necessarily the first arrivals. Represent a tag as \((\text{activation},\text{iteration})\), with an activation shared by its branches but distinct across independent calls. The following trace assumes a matching store that accepts arrivals in any order, exactly one contribution per port and tag, and no requirement that completed joins publish in tag order.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Arrival & Partial matches after arrival & Output tags \\
\hline
Left, tag 1 & Left 1 & none \\
Right, tag 2 & Left 1; right 2 & none \\
Left, tag 2 & Left 1 & 2 \\
Right, tag 1 & none & 2, 1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
on_arrival(port, tag, value):
    record := partial.get_or_create(tag)
    require record[port] is missing
    record[port] := value
    if record.has_left and record.has_right:
        pair := (record.left, record.right)
        if can_publish(tag):
            publish_once(tag, pair)
            remove partial[tag]
\end{verbatim}

If publication may block, retain the complete record and retry publication without replaying the arrival. In a concurrent implementation, installing a contribution and claiming the complete record must be coordinated. A bounded store needs an admission or credit policy; evicting a live partial match is not a legal cache miss. If the interface promises output order, add a separate reorder stage and make it part of the memory budget. For a regression, give both tags distinct arithmetic operands and send the right channel in reverse order. Correct output values prove that tags survived matching; correct tag order alone would not catch swapped operands.

\paragraph{Specify when a tag can be recycled.}
Removing a partial-match record is not enough to make a tag reusable.
An old contribution might still be in flight on either port. The simple
sketch assumes producers send once and no contribution arrives after its
tag completes. If the transport retries messages or reuses activation
numbers, attach a generation or reject stale messages using a retirement
protocol. A generation field affects equality and therefore must survive
serialization through every edge of the graph. A counterexample fixture
starts two successive requests with equal user-visible identifiers, but
delivers one stale response between them; no new match should consume it.
